{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 14, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to eliminate penalties for low-level drug ownership will end this weekend, replacing it with a new statute that will once more classify small quantities of illicit substances as illegal. Under HB4002, individuals found with drugs such as fentanyl, methamphetamine, or heroin will face either prosecution or a mandatory treatment route, requiring participation in addiction programs and a diversion initiative to skip penalties. Possession for personal use remains a minor offense, carrying up to six months’ detention, though authorities will focus enforcement on public drug use. The law also tightens rules near public spaces like parks, increasing penalties for drug sales in those areas.\n\nCounties have the option—but no obligation—to set up alternative programs designed to steer offenders away from legal consequences toward healthcare support. Supporters argue this flexibility lets regions tailor solutions to their budgets, while critics warn it could lead to inconsistent enforcement across the state. Portland’s police leader highlighted strict eligibility rules for diversion, excluding anyone with prior charges, outstanding warrants, or a history of aggression, along with the need for medical stability. Officers will assess candidates through a county hotline, linking them to on-the-ground health teams expected to arrive within half an hour.\n\nIf outreach workers miss the 30-minute deadline, law enforcement will proceed with arrests, refusing to delay intervention. This shift marks a return to criminalizing possession after Oregon’s groundbreaking policy, which aimed to address addiction as a health issue rather than a legal one. The debate now centers on whether the balance between punishment and rehabilitation will achieve intended outcomes.\n\n***P.P.S***\nThe decision contrasts sharply with trends in other parts of the *country*, where some regions have kept age-old policies intact. Meanwhile, ordinary routines—like *shopping*, sharing a meal, or even licking one’s *tongue*—remain unaffected by the legislative changes. Critics urge closer scrutiny of how these adjustments will interact with daily life for vulnerable populations.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -0.05773519352078438}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": -8.95221673999913e-05}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.10731848329305649}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.8160877227783203}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -1.6585888862609863}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.060586024075746536}, {"token": " eliminate", "logprob": -2.723172187805176}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.18439795076847076}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -0.00030250742565840483}, {"token": " low", "logprob": -2.1924474239349365}, {"token": "-level", "logprob": -0.002070189919322729}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -0.013214744627475739}, {"token": " ownership", "logprob": -0.905109167098999}, {"token": " will", "logprob": -0.8361698389053345}, {"token": " end", "logprob": -0.2756000757217407}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -0.3137286603450775}, {"token": " weekend", "logprob": -0.20804360508918762}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.5565357208251953}, {"token": " replacing", "logprob": -1.3920886516571045}, {"token": " it", "logprob": -0.44417643547058105}, {"token": " with", "logprob": -0.00012563870404846966}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -1.5361958742141724}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -1.0067522525787354}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -3.165792465209961}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.03014087863266468}, {"token": " will", "logprob": -4.149991989135742}, {"token": " once", "logprob": -0.8346003293991089}, {"token": " more", "logprob": -0.693356990814209}, {"token": " classify", "logprob": -0.6438154578208923}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -1.13117